"use strict";(self["webpackChunkchinastroll"]=self["webpackChunkchinastroll"]||[]).push([[689],{3689:function(e,n,t){t.r(n),n["default"]='# Returning to Longji with a Drone Pilot’s Eye\n\n![Longji Terraces Return](/images/stories/longji-terraces-return.svg)\n\nI used to pilot delivery drones above Shenzhen’s glittering skyline. My days were measured in firmware updates, altitude logs, and cafeteria coffee. Yet every time my screen showed rain clouds over Guangxi, I thought about the Longji rice terraces—those concentric hills my Zhuang grandparents carved by hand. In 2024 I finally resigned, packed my drones, and rode an overnight train west to see whether technology could protect the terraces that raised me.\n\n## First Steps Back Home\n\nMy village, Ping’an, hadn’t changed much: wooden stilt houses, smoke from bamboo kitchens, and the layered curves of paddies embracing every ridge. What had changed were the climate patterns. Rainstorms arrived in violent bursts, then disappeared for weeks. Landslides swallowed footpaths. Yield charts pinned inside the cooperative office showed a 12% drop since 2020.\n\nChief agronomist Auntie Feng hugged me and whispered, *"If you can teach our teenagers to fly those machines, maybe they can stay instead of leaving."* That sentence became my mission.\n\n## Building the Terrace Flight School\n\nI set up a workshop in an empty granary using the laptops and LiDAR drones I’d saved from Shenzhen.\n\n1. **Mapping the terraces:** We flew predawn missions to capture centimeter-level elevation models. Elders annotated the maps with folklore—where springs emerge, which stones mark clan boundaries.\n2. **Irrigation alerts:** Using NDVI (Normalized Difference Vegetation Index), we set up color-coded dashboards so farmers could see which paddies needed water before seedlings wilted.\n3. **Slide watch:** We installed soil-moisture probes on slopes with a history of erosion. Drones checked the sensors twice daily, sending WeChat alerts if readings spiked.\n\nWithin three months, 14 teenagers earned remote pilot licenses. Instead of migrating to coastal factories, they formed Longji’s first youth monitoring team.\n\n## Reimagining Tourism\n\nThe pandemic taught us to seek smaller, slower groups. I partnered with neighboring villages to create "Terrace Reset" weekends:\n\n- **Dawn flights:** Visitors join the drone crew at 5 a.m., wearing headsets to see real-time aerial views while farmers explain contour farming.\n- **Soil labs:** Guests test pH levels using bamboo pipettes and log data into the cooperative’s open-source map.\n- **Night salons:** Grandparents tell irrigation legends while teenagers project drone footage onto house walls, turning storytelling into immersive cinema.\n\nWe cap each weekend at 12 guests. The fee funds terrace maintenance and scholarships; guests leave with an understanding that scenic landscapes require constant labor.\n\n## Measuring Impact\n\n| Indicator | 2023 | 2024/25 pilot |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Youth out-migration from Ping’an | 47% | 29% |\n| Average rice yield per mu | 540 kg | 610 kg |\n| Landslide incidents requiring evacuation | 5 | 1 |\n| Tourist nights spent in village | 2,800 | 3,650 |\n\nThe numbers matter because they prove tradition and tech can reinforce each other. My grandfather grins when he sees drones hover above the terraces he once planted barefoot. *"The birds are back,"* he jokes. *"Only now they listen to you."*\n\n## Lessons from the Terraces\n\n- Technology gains legitimacy when elders co-design the workflow.\n- Beauty is maintenance; visitors should pay for the upkeep of the scenes they photograph.\n- Climate adaptation is a local story before it becomes a global headline.\n\nI still miss Shenzhen coffee sometimes, but each time morning fog lifts above Longji and reveals new contour lines, I know I chose the right flight path.\n\n[Back to Stories](/#/stories)\n'}}]);
